# Right-sizing

# Queries slow and spilling to disk ->
# not enough memory per query -> scale UP to a bigger size.

# Queries waiting in a queue before starting ->
# not enough slots for concurrent work -> scale OUT to more clusters.

# A bigger warehouse gives each query more memory.
# It does almost nothing for how many can run at once.
# What you actually want is MAX_CLUSTER_COUNT above 1,
# so Snowflake adds clusters under load and suspends them after
# You pay for that capacity only while it's in use.

# REMEMBER for ingestion:
# Split files into smaller files 100-250 MB size.
# Multiple threads are more efficient - smaller files can be processed in parallel.
# Node = 8 threads. XS is 2 nodes